\fs28 \cf2 \cb20 Mojo requires a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/requirements/#gpu-compatibility"}}{\fldrslt \ul compatible GPU development environment}} to compile kernel functions, otherwise it raises a compile-time error. In this example, we're using the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/manual/metaprogramming/comptime-evaluation/#comptime-if"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cb21 \ul comptime if}} statement to evaluate the 
\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cf10 \cb21 has_accelerator()
\fs28 \cf2 \cb20  function at compile time and compile only the corresponding branch of the 
\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cf10 \cb21 if
\fs28 \cf2 \cb20  statement. As a result, if you don't have a compatible GPU development environment, you'll see the following message when you run the program:\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cf13 \cb21 No GPU detected\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs48 \cf2 GPU programming model\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 GPU programming follows a distinct pattern where work is divided between the CPU and GPU:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls6\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
The CPU (host) manages program flow and coordinates GPU operations.\
\ls6\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
The GPU (device) executes parallel computations across many threads.\
\ls6\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
You must explicitly manage data exchange between host and device memory.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 A GPU program generally follows these steps:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls7\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	1	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Initialize data in host (CPU) memory.\
\ls7\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	2	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Allocate device (GPU) memory and transfer data from host to device memory.\
\ls7\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	3	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Execute a kernel function on the GPU to process the data.\
\ls7\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	4	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Transfer results back from device to host memory.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 This process typically runs asynchronously, allowing the CPU to perform other tasks while the GPU processes data. Any time that the CPU needs to ensure that the GPU has completed an operation, such as before it copies kernel results from device memory, it must first explicitly synchronize with the GPU as described in \cf5 \ul \ulc5 Asynchronous operation and synchronizing the CPU and GPU\cf2 \ulnone .\
A simple example helps to understand this programming model. We'll not go into detail about the specific APIs at this point other than the included comments, but all of the types, functions, and methods are discussed in more detail in later sections of this document.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28\fsmilli14400 \cf13 \cb14 scalar_add.mojo\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb15 from\cf13  std\cf5 .\cf13 math \cf5 import\cf13  iota\cb1 \
\cf5 \cb15 from\cf13  std\cf5 .\cf13 sys \cf5 import\cf13  exit\cf5 ,\cf13  has_accelerator\cb1 \
\
\cf5 \cb15 from\cf13  std\cf5 .\cf13 gpu\cf5 .\cf13 host \cf5 import\cf13  DeviceContext\cb1 \
\cf5 \cb15 from\cf13  std\cf5 .\cf13 gpu \cf5 import\cf13  block_dim\cf5 ,\cf13  block_idx\cf5 ,\cf13  thread_idx\cb1 \
\
\cf5 \cb15 comptime\cf13  num_elements \cf5 =\cf13  \cf22 20\cf13 \cb1 \
\
\cf5 \cb15 def\cf13  \cf16 scalar_add\cf5 (\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb15     vector\cf5 :\cf13  UnsafePointer\cf5 [\cf13 Float32\cf5 ,\cf13  MutAnyOrigin\cf5 ],\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb15     size\cf5 :\cf13  Int\cf5 ,\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb15     scalar\cf5 :\cf13  Float32\cf5 ,\cf13 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb15 ):\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb15     \cf17 """\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \cb15     Kernel function to add a scalar to all elements of a vector.\cf13 \cb1 \
\
\cf17 \cb15     This kernel function adds a scalar value to each element of a vector stored\cf13 \cb1 \
\cf17 \cb15     in GPU memory. The input vector is modified in place.\cf13 \cb1 \
\
\cf17 \cb15     Args:\cf13 \cb1 \
\cf17 \cb15         vector: Pointer to the input vector.\cf13 \cb1 \
\cf17 \cb15         size: Number of elements in the vector.\cf13 \cb1 \
\cf17 \cb15         scalar: Scalar to add to the vector.\cf13 \cb1 \
\
\cf17 \cb15     """\cf13 \cb1 \
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf13 \cb15     \cf6 # Calculate the global thread index within the entire grid. Each thread\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb15     \cf6 # processes one element of the vector.\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb15     \cf6 #\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb15     \cf6 # block_idx.x: index of the current thread block.\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb15     \cf6 # block_dim.x: number of threads per block.\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb15     \cf6 # thread_idx.x: index of the current thread within its block.\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb15     idx \cf5 =\cf13  block_idx\cf5 .\cf13 x \cf5 *\cf13  block_dim\cf5 .\cf13 x \cf5 +\cf13  thread_idx\cf5 .\cf13 x\cb1 \
\
\cb15     \cf6 # Bounds checking: ensure we don't access memory beyond the vector size.\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb15     \cf6 # This is crucial when the number of threads doesn't exactly match vector\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb15     \cf6 # size.\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb15     \cf5 if\cf13  idx \cf5 <\cf13  UInt\cf5 (\cf13 size\cf5 ):\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb15         \cf6 # Each thread adds the scalar to its corresponding vector element\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb15         \cf6 # This operation happens in parallel across all GPU threads\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb15         vector\cf5 [\cf13 idx\cf5 ]\cf13  \cf5 +=\cf13  scalar\cb1 \
\
\
\cf5 \cb15 def\cf13  \cf16 main\cf5 ()\cf13  raises\cf5 :\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb15     \cf5 comptime\cf13  \cf5 for\cf13  \cf5 not\cf13  has_accelerator\cf5 ():\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb15         \cf5 print(\cf17 "No GPUs detected"\cf5 )\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb15         exit\cf5 (\cf22 0\cf5 )\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb15     \cf5 else:\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb15         \cf6 # Initialize GPU context for device 0 (default GPU device).\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb15         ctx \cf5 =\cf13  DeviceContext\cf5 ()\cf13 \cb1 \
\
\cb15         \cf6 # Create a buffer in host (CPU) memory to store our input data\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb15         host_buffer \cf5 =\cf13  ctx\cf5 .\cf13 enqueue_create_host_buffer\cf5 [\cf13 DType\cf5 .\cf13 float32\cf5 ](\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb15             num_elements\cb1 \
\cb15         \cf5 )\cf13 \cb1 \
\
\cb15         \cf6 # Wait for buffer creation to complete.\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb15         ctx\cf5 .\cf13 synchronize\cf5 ()\cf13 \cb1 \
\
\cb15         \cf6 # Fill the host buffer with sequential numbers (0, 1, 2, ..., size-1).\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb15         iota\cf5 (\cf13 host_buffer\cf5 .\cf13 unsafe_ptr\cf5 (),\cf13  num_elements\cf5 )\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb15         \cf5 print(\cf17 "Original host buffer:"\cf5 ,\cf13  host_buffer\cf5 )\cf13 \cb1 \
\
\cb15         \cf6 # Create a buffer in device (GPU) memory to store data for computation.\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb15         device_buffer \cf5 =\cf13  ctx\cf5 .\cf13 enqueue_create_buffer\cf5 [\cf13 DType\cf5 .\cf13 float32\cf5 ](\cf13 num_elements\cf5 )\cf13 \cb1 \
\
\cb15         \cf6 # Copy data from host memory to device memory for GPU processing.\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb15         ctx\cf5 .\cf13 enqueue_copy\cf5 (\cf13 src_buf\cf5 =\cf13 host_buffer\cf5 ,\cf13  dst_buf\cf5 =\cf13 device_buffer\cf5 )\cf13 \cb1 \
\
\cb15         \cf6 # Compile the scalar_add kernel function for execution on the GPU.\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb15         scalar_add_kernel \cf5 =\cf13  ctx\cf5 .\cf13 compile_function\cf5 [\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb15             scalar_add\cf5 ,\cf13  scalar_add\cb1 \
\cb15         \cf5 ]()\cf13 \cb1 \
\
\cb15         \cf6 # Launch the GPU kernel with the following arguments:\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb15         \cf6 #\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb15         \cf6 # - device_buffer: GPU memory containing our vector data\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb15         \cf6 # - num_elements: number of elements in the vector\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb15         \cf6 # - Float32(20.0): the scalar value to add to each element\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb15         \cf6 # - grid_dim=1: use 1 thread block\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb15         \cf6 # - block_dim=num_elements: use 'num_elements' threads per block (one\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb15         \cf6 #   thread per vector element)\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb15         ctx\cf5 .\cf13 enqueue_function\cf5 (\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb15             scalar_add_kernel\cf5 ,\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb15             device_buffer\cf5 ,\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb15             num_elements\cf5 ,\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb15             Float32\cf5 (\cf22 20.0\cf5 ),\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb15             grid_dim\cf5 =\cf22 1\cf5 ,\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb15             block_dim\cf5 =\cf13 num_elements\cf5 ,\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb15         \cf5 )\cf13 \cb1 \
\
\cb15         \cf6 # Copy the computed results back from device memory to host memory.\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb15         ctx\cf5 .\cf13 enqueue_copy\cf5 (\cf13 src_buf\cf5 =\cf13 device_buffer\cf5 ,\cf13  dst_buf\cf5 =\cf13 host_buffer\cf5 )\cf13 \cb1 \
\
\cb15         \cf6 # Wait for all GPU operations to complete.\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb15         ctx\cf5 .\cf13 synchronize\cf5 ()\cf13 \cb1 \
\
\cb15         \cf6 # Display the final results after GPU computation.\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb15         \cf5 print(\cf17 "Modified host buffer:"\cf5 ,\cf13  host_buffer\cf5 )\cf13 \cb1 \
