\fs64 \cf2 Accessing and managing GPUs with 
\fs56 \cf10 \cb12 DeviceContext
\fs64 \cf2 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 The {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/gpu/host/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul gpu.host}} package includes the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/gpu/host/device_context/DeviceContext/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul DeviceContext}} struct, which represents a logical instance of a GPU device. It provides methods for allocating memory on the device, copying data between the host CPU and the GPU, and compiling and running functions (also known as kernels) on the device.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs48 \cf2 Creating an instance of 
\fs42 \cf10 \cb12 DeviceContext
\fs48 \cf2 \cb1  to access a GPU\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Mojo supports systems with multiple GPUs. GPUs are uniquely identified by integer indices starting with 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 0
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , which is considered the "default" device. You can determine the number of GPUs available by invoking the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/gpu/host/device_context/DeviceContext/#number_of_devices"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul DeviceContext.number_of_devices()}} static method.\
The 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 DeviceContext()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  constructor returns an instance for interacting with a specified GPU. It accepts two optional arguments:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls8\ilvl0
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
device_id
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 : An integer index of a specific GPU on the system. The default value of 0 refers to the "default" GPU for the system.\
\ls8\ilvl0
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
api
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 : A 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 String
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  specifying a particular vendor's API. "cuda" (NVIDIA), "hip" (AMD), and "metal" (Apple) are currently supported.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 If your system doesn't have a supported GPU\'97or doesn't have a GPU matching the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 device_id
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  or 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 api
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , if provided\'97then the constructor raises an error.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs48 \cf2 Asynchronous operation and synchronizing the CPU and GPU\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Typical CPU-GPU interaction is asynchronous, allowing the GPU to process tasks while the CPU is busy with other work. Each 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 DeviceContext
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  has an associated stream of queued operations to execute on the GPU. Operations within a stream execute in the order they are enqueued.\
The {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/gpu/host/device_context/DeviceContext/#synchronize"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul synchronize()}} method blocks execution of the current CPU thread until all queued operations on the associated 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 DeviceContext
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  stream have completed. Most commonly, you use this to wait until the result of a kernel function is copied from device memory to host memory before accessing it on the host.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa800\partightenfactor0

\fs64 \cf2 Kernel functions\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 A GPU kernel is simply a function that runs on a GPU, executing a specific computation on a large dataset in parallel across thousands or millions of threads. You specify the number of threads when you execute a kernel function, and all threads run the same kernel function. However, the GPU assigns a unique thread index for each thread, and you use the thread index to determine which data elements an individual thread should process.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs48 \cf2 Multidimensional grids and thread organization\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 As discussed in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/manual/gpu/architecture/#gpu-execution-model"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul GPU execution model}}, a grid is the top-level organizational structure of the threads executing a kernel function on a GPU. A grid consists of multiple thread blocks, which are organized across one, two, or three dimensions. Each thread block is further divided into individual threads, which are in turn organized across one, two, or three dimensions.\
You specify the grid and thread block dimensions with the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 grid_dim
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  and 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 block_dim
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  keyword arguments when you enqueue a kernel function to execute using the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/gpu/host/device_context/DeviceContext/#enqueue_function"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul enqueue_function()}} method. For example:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf6 \cb15 # Enqueue the print_threads() kernel function\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf13 \cb15 ctx\cf5 .\cf13 enqueue_function\cf5 [\cf13 print_threads\cf5 ,\cf13  print_threads\cf5 ](\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb15     grid_dim\cf5 =(\cf22 2\cf5 ,\cf13  \cf22 2\cf5 ,\cf13  \cf22 1\cf5 ),\cf13    \cf6 # 2x2x1 blocks per grid\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb15     block_dim\cf5 =(\cf22 4\cf5 ,\cf13  \cf22 4\cf5 ,\cf13  \cf22 2\cf5 ),\cf13   \cf6 # 4x4x2 threads per block\cf13 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb15 )\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf19 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 For both 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 grid_dim
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  and 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 block_dim
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , you express the size in the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 x
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 y
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , and 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 z
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  dimensions as a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/gpu/host/dim/Dim/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul Dim}} or a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Tuple
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 . The 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 y
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  and 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 z
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  dimensions default to 1 if you don't explicitly provide them (that is, 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 (2, 2)
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  is treated as 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 (2, 2, 1)
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  and 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 (8,)
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  is treated as 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 (8, 1, 1)
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 ). You can also provide just an 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Int
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  value to specify only the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 x
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  dimension (that is, 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 64
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  is treated as 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 (64, 1, 1)
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 ).\
