\fs28 \cf2 \cb20 Printing from within a kernel function is not currently supported on Apple silicon GPUs.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs48 \cf2 Writing a kernel function\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Kernel functions must be {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/manual/functions/#raising-and-non-raising-functions"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul non-raising}}.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf5 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf2 \cb20 For functions called from GPU kernels that need error handling, Mojo supports typed errors with types that don't allocate heap memory. See {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/manual/errors/#define-a-custom-error-type"}}{\fldrslt \ul Define a custom error type}} for details.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Argument values must be of types that conform to the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/builtin/device_passable/DevicePassable/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul DevicePassable}} trait. Additionally, a kernel function can't have a return value. Instead, you must write any result of a kernel function to a memory buffer passed in as an argument. The next two sections, \cf5 \ul Passing data between CPU and GPU\cf2 \ulnone  and 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul DeviceBuffer
\fs32 \cb1  and 
\fs28 \cb12 HostBuffer
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 \ulnone  go into more detail on how to pass values to a kernel function and get back results.\
As discussed in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/manual/gpu/architecture/#gpu-execution-model"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul GPU execution model}}, when the GPU executes a kernel, it assigns the grid's thread blocks to various streaming multiprocessors (SMs) for execution. The SM then divides the thread block into subsets of threads called a warp. The size of a warp depends on the GPU architecture, but most modern GPUs currently use a warp size of 32 or 64 threads.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 {{\NeXTGraphic Attachment.png \width320 \height320 \appleattachmentpadding0 \appleembedtype0 \appleaqc
}¬}\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs27\fsmilli13600 \cf2 Figure 2. Hierarchy of threads running on a GPU, showing the relationship of the grid, thread blocks, warps, and individual threads, based on HIP Programming Guide \'a92023-2025 Advanced Micro Devices, Inc.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 If a thread block contains a number of threads not evenly divisible by the warp size, the SM creates a partially filled final warp that still consumes the full warp's resources. For example, if a thread block has 100 threads and the warp size is 32, the SM creates:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls9\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
3 full warps of 32 threads each (96 threads total).\
\ls9\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
1 partial warp with only 4 active threads but still occupying a full warp's worth of resources (32 thread slots).\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Because of this execution model, you must ensure that the threads in your kernel don't attempt to access out-of-bounds data. Otherwise, your kernel might crash or produce incorrect results. For example, if you pass a 2,000-element vector to a kernel that you execute with single-dimension thread blocks of 512 threads each, and each thread is responsible for processing one element, your kernel could perform a boundary check like this to ensure that it doesn't attempt to process out-of-bounds elements:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb15 from\cf13  std\cf5 .\cf13 gpu \cf5 import\cf13  global_idx\cb1 \
\
\cf5 \cb15 def\cf13  \cf16 process_vector\cf5 (\cf13 vector\cf5 :\cf13  UnsafePointer\cf5 [\cf13 Float32\cf5 ,\cf13  MutAnyOrigin\cf5 ],\cf13  size\cf5 :\cf13  Int\cf5 ):\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb15     \cf5 if\cf13  global_idx\cf5 .\cf13 x \cf5 <\cf13  size\cf5 :\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb15         \cf6 # Process vector[global_idx.x] in some way\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

