\fs48 \cf2 Passing data between CPU and GPU\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 All values passed to a kernel function must be of types that conform to the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/builtin/device_passable/DevicePassable/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul DevicePassable}} trait. The trait declares a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/manual/traits/#comptime-members-for-generics"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul comptime
\fs32 \cb1  member}} named 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 device_type
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  that maps the type as used on the CPU host to a corresponding type used on the GPU device.\
As an example, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/gpu/host/device_context/DeviceBuffer/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul DeviceBuffer}} is a host-side representation of a buffer located in the GPU's global memory space. But it defines its 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 device_type
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  member as 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 UnsafePointer
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , so the data represented by a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 DeviceBuffer
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  is actually passed to the kernel function as a value of type {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/memory/unsafe_pointer/UnsafePointer/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul UnsafePointer}}. The next section, 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul DeviceBuffer
\fs32 \cb1  and 
\fs28 \cb12 HostBuffer
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 \ulnone , describes in more detail how to allocate memory buffers on the host and device and to exchange blocks of data between host and device.\
The following table lists the most commonly used types in the Mojo libraries that conform to the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 DevicePassable
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  trait.\

