\fs42 \cf10 \cb12 DeviceBuffer
\fs48 \cf2 \cb1  and 
\fs42 \cf10 \cb12 HostBuffer
\fs48 \cf2 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 This section describes how to use 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 DeviceBuffer
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  and 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 HostBuffer
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  to allocate memory on the device and host respectively, and to copy data between device and host memory.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs40 \cf2 Creating a 
\fs35 \cf10 \cb12 DeviceBuffer
\fs40 \cf2 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 The {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/gpu/host/device_context/DeviceBuffer/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul DeviceBuffer}} type represents a block of device memory associated with a particular 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 DeviceContext
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 . Specifically, the buffer is located in the device's global memory space. As such, the buffer is accessible by all threads of all kernel functions executed by the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 DeviceContext
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 .\
As discussed in \cf5 \ul Passing data between CPU and GPU\cf2 \ulnone , 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 DeviceBuffer
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  is the type used by the host to allocate the buffer and to copy data between the host and device. But when you pass a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 DeviceBuffer
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  to a kernel function, the argument received by the function is of type 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 UnsafePointer
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 . Attempting to use the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 DeviceBuffer
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  type directly from within a kernel function results in an error.\
The {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/gpu/host/device_context/DeviceContext/#enqueue_create_buffer"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul DeviceContext.enqueue_create_buffer()}} method creates a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 DeviceBuffer
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  associated with that 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 DeviceContext
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 . It accepts the data type as a compile-time {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/builtin/dtype/DType/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul DType}} parameter and the size of the buffer as a run-time argument. So to create a buffer for 1,024 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/builtin/simd/#float32"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul Float32}} values, you would execute:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf13 \cb15 device_buffer \cf5 =\cf13  ctx\cf5 .\cf13 enqueue_create_buffer\cf5 [\cf13 DType\cf5 .\cf13 float32\cf5 ](\cf22 1024\cf5 )\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf19 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 As the method name implies, this method is asynchronous and enqueues the operation on the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 DeviceContext
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 's associated \cf5 \ul stream of queued operations\cf2 \ulnone .\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs40 \cf2 Creating a 
\fs35 \cf10 \cb12 HostBuffer
\fs40 \cf2 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 The {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/gpu/host/device_context/HostBuffer/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul HostBuffer}} type is analogous to 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 DeviceBuffer
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , but represents a block of host memory associated with a particular 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 DeviceContext
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 . It supports methods for transferring data between host and device memory, as well as a basic set of methods for accessing data elements by index and for printing the buffer.\
The {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/gpu/host/device_context/DeviceContext/#enqueue_create_host_buffer"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul DeviceContext.enqueue_create_host_buffer()}} method accepts the data type as a compile-time {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/builtin/dtype/DType/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul DType}} parameter and the size of the buffer as a run-time argument and returns a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 HostBuffer
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 . As with all 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 DeviceContext
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  methods whose name starts with 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 enqueue_
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , the method is asynchronous and returns immediately, adding the operation to the queue to be executed by the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 DeviceContext
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 . Therefore, you need to call the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 synchronize()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  method to ensure that the operation has completed before you write to or read from the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 HostBuffer
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  object.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf13 \cb15 device_buffer \cf5 =\cf13  ctx\cf5 .\cf13 enqueue_create_host_buffer\cf5 [\cf13 DType\cf5 .\cf13 float32\cf5 ](\cf22 1024\cf5 )\cf13 \cb1 \
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf6 \cb15 # Synchronize to wait until buffer is created before attempting to write to it\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf13 \cb15 ctx\cf5 .\cf13 synchronize\cf5 ()\cf13 \cb1 \
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf6 \cb15 # Now it's safe to write to the buffer\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf5 \cb15 for\cf13  i \cf5 in\cf13  \cf16 range\cf5 (\cf22 1024\cf5 ):\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb15     device_buffer\cf5 [\cf13 i\cf5 ]\cf13  \cf5 =\cf13  Float32\cf5 (\cf13 i \cf5 *\cf13  i\cf5 )\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf19 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs40 \cf2 Copying data between host and device memory\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 The {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/gpu/host/device_context/DeviceContext/#enqueue_copy"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul enqueue_copy()}} method is overloaded to support copying from host to device, device to host, or even device to device for systems that have multiple GPUs. Typically, you'll use it to copy data that you've staged in a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 HostBuffer
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  to a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 DeviceBuffer
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  before executing a kernel, and then from a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 DeviceBuffer
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  to a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 HostBuffer
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  to retrieve the results of kernel execution. The 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 scalar_add.mojo
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  example in \cf5 \ul GPU programming model\cf2 \ulnone  shows this pattern in action. In it, the kernel function does an in-place modification of the buffer it receives as an argument and then reuses the original 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 HostBuffer
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  to copy the results back from the device. However, you can allocate a separate 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 DeviceBuffer
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  and 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 HostBuffer
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  for the result of a kernel function if you want to retain the original data.\
In addition to copying data between a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 HostBuffer
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  to a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 DeviceBuffer
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , you can use an {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/memory/unsafe_pointer/UnsafePointer/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul UnsafePointer}} as the source or destination of a copy. However, the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 UnsafePointer
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  must reference host memory for this operation. Attempting to use an 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 UnsafePointer
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  referencing device memory results in an error. For example, this is useful if you have data already staged in a data structure on the host that can expose the data through an 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 UnsafePointer
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 . In that case you would not need to copy the data from the data structure to a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 HostBuffer
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  before copying it to the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 DeviceBuffer
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 .\
Both 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 DeviceBuffer
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  and 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 HostBuffer
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  also include {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/gpu/host/device_context/DeviceBuffer/#enqueue_copy_to"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul enqueue_copy_to()}} and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/gpu/host/device_context/DeviceBuffer/#enqueue_copy_from"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul enqueue_copy_from()}} methods. These are simply convenience methods that call the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 enqueue_copy()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  method on their corresponding 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 DeviceContext
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 . For example, the following two method calls are interchangeable:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf13 \cb15 ctx\cf5 .\cf13 enqueue_copy\cf5 (\cf13 src_buf\cf5 =\cf13 host_buffer\cf5 ,\cf13  dst_buf\cf5 =\cf13 device_buffer\cf5 )\cf13 \cb1 \
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf6 \cb15 # Equivalent to:\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf13 \cb15 host_buffer\cf5 .\cf13 enqueue_copy_to\cf5 (\cf13 dst\cf5 =\cf13 device_buffer\cf5 )\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf19 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Finally, as a convenience for testing or prototyping, you can use the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/gpu/host/device_context/DeviceBuffer/#map_to_host"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul DeviceBuffer.map_to_host()}} method to create a host-accessible view of the device buffer's contents. This returns 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 HostBuffer
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  as a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/manual/errors/#use-a-context-manager"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul context manager}} that contains a copy of the data from the corresponding 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 DeviceBuffer
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 . Additionally, any modifications that you make to the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 HostBuffer
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  are automatically copied back to the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 DeviceBuffer
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  when the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 with
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  statement exits. For example:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf13 \cb15 ctx \cf5 =\cf13  DeviceContext\cf5 ()\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb15 length \cf5 =\cf13  \cf22 1024\cf13 \cb1 \
\
\cb15 input_device \cf5 =\cf13  ctx\cf5 .\cf13 enqueue_create_buffer\cf5 [\cf13 DType\cf5 .\cf13 float32\cf5 ](\cf13 length\cf5 )\cf13 \cb1 \
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf6 \cb15 # Initialize the input\cf13 \cb1 \
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf5 \cb15 with\cf13  input_device\cf5 .\cf13 map_to_host\cf5 ()\cf13  \cf5 as\cf13  input_host\cf5 :\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb15     \cf5 for\cf13  i \cf5 in\cf13  \cf16 range\cf5 (\cf13 length\cf5 ):\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb15         input_host\cf5 [\cf13 i\cf5 ]\cf13  \cf5 =\cf13  Float32\cf5 (\cf13 i\cf5 )\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf19 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 However, you should not use this in most production code because of the bidirectional copies and synchronization. The example above is equivalent to:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf13 \cb15 ctx \cf5 =\cf13  DeviceContext\cf5 ()\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb15 length \cf5 =\cf13  \cf22 1024\cf13 \cb1 \
\
\cb15 input_device \cf5 =\cf13  ctx\cf5 .\cf13 enqueue_create_buffer\cf5 [\cf13 DType\cf5 .\cf13 float32\cf5 ](\cf13 length\cf5 )\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb15 input_host \cf5 =\cf13  ctx\cf5 .\cf13 enqueue_create_host_buffer\cf5 [\cf13 DType\cf5 .\cf13 float32\cf5 ](\cf13 length\cf5 )\cf13 \cb1 \
\
\cb15 input_device\cf5 .\cf13 enqueue_copy_to\cf5 (\cf13 input_host\cf5 )\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb15 ctx\cf5 .\cf13 synchronize\cf5 ()\cf13 \cb1 \
\
\cf5 \cb15 for\cf13  i \cf5 in\cf13  \cf16 range\cf5 (\cf13 length\cf5 ):\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb15     input_host\cf5 [\cf13 i\cf5 ]\cf13  \cf5 =\cf13  Float32\cf5 (\cf13 i\cf5 )\cf13 \cb1 \
\
\cb15 input_host\cf5 .\cf13 enqueue_copy_to\cf5 (\cf13 input_device\cf5 )\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb15 ctx\cf5 .\cf13 synchronize\cf5 ()\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf19 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs40 \cf2 Deallocating memory buffers\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Both 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 DeviceBuffer
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  and 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 HostBuffer
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  are subject to Mojo's standard ownership and lifecycle mechanisms. The Mojo compiler analyzes our program to determine the last point that the owner of or a reference to an object is used and automatically adds a call to the object's destructor. This means that you don't explicitly call any method to free the memory represented by a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 DeviceBuffer
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  or 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 HostBuffer
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  instance. See the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/manual/values/ownership/"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul Ownership}} and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/manual/lifecycle/"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul Intro to value lifecycle}} sections of the Mojo Manual for more information on Mojo value ownership and value lifecycle management, and the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/manual/lifecycle/death/"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul Death of a value}} section for a detailed explanation of object destruction.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs48 \cf2 Compiling and enqueuing a kernel function for execution\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 The {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/gpu/host/device_context/DeviceContext/#compile_function"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul compile_function()}} method accepts a kernel function as a compile-time {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/manual/parameters/"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul parameter}} and then compiles it for the associated 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 DeviceContext
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 . Then you can enqueue the compiled kernel for execution by passing it to the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/gpu/host/device_context/DeviceContext/#enqueue_function"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul enqueue_function()}} method. The example in the \cf5 \ul GPU programming model\cf2 \ulnone  demonstrated this pattern:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28\fsmilli14400 \cf13 \cb14 scalar_add.mojo\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb15 ...\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb15 scalar_add_kernel \cf5 =\cf13  ctx\cf5 .\cf13 compile_function\cf5 [\cf13 scalar_add\cf5 ,\cf13  scalar_add\cf5 ]()\cf13 \cb1 \
\
\cb15 ctx\cf5 .\cf13 enqueue_function\cf5 (\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb15     scalar_add_kernel\cf5 ,\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb15     device_buffer\cf5 ,\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb15     num_elements\cf5 ,\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb15     Float32\cf5 (\cf22 20.0\cf5 ),\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb15     grid_dim\cf5 =\cf22 1\cf5 ,\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb15     block_dim\cf5 =\cf13 num_elements\cf5 ,\cf13 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb15 )\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf19 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 When using a compiled kernel function like this, you execute it by calling 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 enqueue_function()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  with the following arguments in this order:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls10\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
The kernel function to execute.\
\ls10\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Any additional arguments specified by the kernel function definition in the order specified by the function.\
\ls10\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
The grid dimensions using the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 grid_dim
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  keyword argument.\
\ls10\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
The thread block dimensions using the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 block_dim
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  keyword argument.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Refer to the \cf5 \ul Multidimensional grids and thread organization\cf2 \ulnone  section for more information on grid and thread block dimensions.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf5 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb20 API variants\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa127\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb20 The 
\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cf10 \cb21 compile_function_unchecked()
\fs28 \cf2 \cb20  and 
\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cf10 \cb21 enqueue_function_unchecked()
\fs28 \cf2 \cb20  methods are deprecated and will be removed in a future release because they don't perform compile-time type checking of the kernel function arguments. You'll encounter run-time errors if the values you pass don't conform to the expected argument types.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb20 The 
\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cf10 \cb21 compile_function_experimental()
\fs28 \cf2 \cb20  and 
\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cf10 \cb21 enqueue_function_experimental()
\fs28 \cf2 \cb20  methods perform compile-time type checking but require the kernel function as a parameter only once instead of twice. Once we verify these APIs work correctly, we'll rename them to 
\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cf10 \cb21 compile_function()
\fs28 \cf2 \cb20  and 
\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cf10 \cb21 enqueue_function()
\fs28 \cf2 \cb20  to make type-checking the default behavior.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 The advantage of compiling the kernel as a separate step is that that you can execute the same compiled kernel on the same device multiple times. This avoids the overhead of compiling the kernel each time it's executed.\
If your application needs to execute a kernel function only once, you can use an overloaded version of 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 enqueue_function()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  that compiles the kernel and enqueues it in a single step. Therefore, the following is equivalent to the separate calls to 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 compile_function()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  and 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 enqueue_function()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  shown above (note that the kernel function is provided as a compile-time parameter in this case):\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf13 \cb15 ctx\cf5 .\cf13 enqueue_function\cf5 [\cf13 scalar_add\cf5 ,\cf13  scalar_add\cf5 ](\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb15     device_buffer\cf5 ,\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb15     num_elements\cf5 ,\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb15     Float32\cf5 (\cf22 20.0\cf5 ),\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb15     grid_dim\cf5 =\cf22 1\cf5 ,\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb15     block_dim\cf5 =\cf13 num_elements\cf5 ,\cf13 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb15 )\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

